# 1Password for Linux custom browser allowlist.
#
# 1Password only speaks to a browser it recognises. Anything not on its built-in
# list needs its binary's filename here, one per line; `#` comments are ignored.
#
# Helium is the daily browser and is not a browser 1Password knows, so without
# this line the extension cannot reach the desktop app at all — and with no
# desktop app there is no system-authentication prompt, which is what puts the
# fingerprint reader in front of an unlock.
#
# The name must match the browser binary's filename, which is why this is bare
# `helium` and not a path: the launcher at ~/.local/bin/helium execs
# /opt/helium/helium-wrapper, which in turn execs /opt/helium/helium.
#
# Naming the browser here is necessary but not sufficient. 1Password also stats
# the binary and refuses it if it looks user-tamperable, so the install has to
# stay root-owned and outside $HOME. An AppImage fails even earlier: it runs
# from a user FUSE mount, and the kernel denies 1Password-BrowserSupport — which
# is setgid `onepassword` — any access to a mount whose gid does not match. See
# the header of ~/.local/bin/helium-update.

helium
